@@ -121,9 +121,10 @@ I(u, θ) = I_tex(u, θ)                              u ≤ R_tex
 The `1/u²` law is exact for a straight edge and pessimistic for everything else, so the
 extrapolated tail is never brighter than the physics; a smooth (round) pupil, whose ring is
 already tiny, extrapolates to nothing. The energy in the extrapolated tail up to the sprite
-radius is `Σ_θ I_ring(θ) · R_tex² · ln(R_sprite / R_tex)` — finite; the CPU adds it to the
-kernel's integral when it normalises (the ring and `ln(R_sprite/R_tex)` are known), so the
-deposit is energy-correct including the tail.
+radius is `Σ_θ I_ring(θ) · R_tex² · ln(R_sprite / R_tex)` — finite. It is **not** normalised
+away: the kernel already carries physical units (§2.4), so the tail is simply extra glare the
+sprite is or is not wide enough to hold. The CPU reports the coefficient so the split can be
+logged.
 
 Wavelength: the pixel shader samples at `n_λ = 6` wavelengths (420, 470, 520, 570, 620, 670 nm),
 each a scaled copy `u_λ = u · λ₀ / λ`, weight `(λ₀/λ)²`, colour from a small RGB table
@@ -147,11 +148,20 @@ monochrome white pattern at 550 nm.
 Exactly the front filter's sparse convolution (`FilterPSFSpec.md` §2–3): the quarter-res HDR
 frame thresholded with the streaks' own threshold (`HDR_Streaks_Threshold`, soft knee `T/4`),
 one sprite per surviving texel, deposit `E · amount · I(u)` additively into `m_pTexHDRFinalBloom`
-divided by `fBloomCompositeWeight`. With the kernel normalised to unit integral (core removed,
-tail included), **`amount = 1` is the physical answer**: the fraction of a source's light that
-lands in its glare is what the pupil says. `amount` is the look knob on top (0–4, default 1);
-no f-number ramp on the component side in Wave mode. `HDR_Streaks_Threshold` keeps its meaning
-and default.
+divided by `fBloomCompositeWeight`.
+
+`I` carries **physical units and is never renormalised**. The full pattern is scaled so
+`Σ I = 1` (Parseval — the pupil's whole transmitted light) *before* the core is removed, so
+what the texture holds integrates to `1 − E_core`, and the extrapolated tail adds
+`T_tail = Σ_θ I_ring(θ) · R_tex² · ln(R_sprite / R_tex)`. **`amount = 1` therefore deposits
+`E · (1 − E_core + T_tail)`, the physical fraction** — about 0.07 of the source for an 8-blade
+iris, typically 5–15 % across pupils. Renormalising the core-removed kernel back to unit
+integral would be wrong twice over: it would hand the pattern the source's *entire* excess
+(~14× too much), and it would make the deposit depend on `R_sprite`, which is a cost choice.
+The only unit conversion the renderer applies is `1 / s_q²`, turning "per kernel texel" into
+"per quarter-resolution pixel". `amount` above 1 is explicitly the **exaggeration side** (0–4,
+default 1 = physical); no f-number ramp on the component side in Wave mode.
+`HDR_Streaks_Threshold` keeps its meaning and default.
 
 Anamorphic squeeze: the iris sits *behind* the cylindrical front group, so its diffraction
 pattern is formed unsqueezed on the sensor and the desqueeze stretches it horizontally by `S`:
@@ -181,7 +191,7 @@ d        = quad-local in [-1, 1] · R_sprite_px                       (native px
 for each λ: u_λ = |d| / s_px · (λ₀ / λ);  θ = atan2(d.y, d.x)
             I_λ = (u_λ ≤ R_tex) ? kernel(u_λ, θ)² : ring(θ)² · (R_tex / u_λ)²
             c  += colour_λ · (λ₀/λ)² · I_λ
-out = Color.rgb · c · norm                                            (norm = 1 / kernel integral incl. tail, from the CPU)
+out = Color.rgb · c · norm                                            (norm = 1 / s_q² only — texel to pixel; the kernel is NOT renormalised)
 ```
 
 `kernel(u, θ)` samples `$DiffractionKernel` at `0.5 + u/L · (cos θ, sin θ)` (bilinear); `ring(θ)`
@@ -195,7 +205,10 @@ choice, documented as such). Cost target: < 0.3 ms GPU at 1080p with a few sourc
 sprite pixel = 6 texture reads + 6 ring reads).
 
 Log once per change: `[BLOOM] diffraction kernel blades=%d exp=%.2f phase=%.0f mask=%s
-maskRot=%.0f maskScale=%.2f sa=%.2f -> core=%.2f tail=%.3f pitch=%.2fpx N=%.1f (job %.1f ms)`.
+maskRot=%.0f maskScale=%.2f sa=%.2f -> core=%.2f tail=%.3f physical glare fraction = %.3f
+L=%d (job %.1f ms)` — the fraction is `1 − E_core`, so how much of the light really becomes
+glare is readable at a glance. The glare pass logs the fraction it actually deposits (tail
+included, `amount` applied).
 
